      "source_text": "In Situation <REF_0001> let <MATH_0001> be a locally Noetherian\nscheme over <MATH_0002>. Let <MATH_0003> be a triple satisfying (1), (2), and\n(3) above. We want to explain a way to think about the compatibility\ncondition (4). It will not be mathematically precise as we are going use\na fictitious category <MATH_0004> of analytic spaces over <MATH_0005>\nand a fictitious analytification functor\n<MATHSEG_0001>locally Noetherian formal<MATHSEG_0002>algebraic spaces over <MATHSEG_0003>\nFor example if <MATH_0006> over <MATH_0007>, then <MATH_0008>\nshould be thought of as an open unit disc. If <MATH_0009>, then <MATH_0010>\nis a single point. The category <MATH_0011> should have open and\nclosed immersions and we should be able to take the open complement\nof a closed. Given <MATH_0012> the morphism <MATH_0013> should induces a\nclosed immersion <MATH_0014>. We set\n<MATH_0015> equal to its open complement.\nIf <MATH_0016> is an algebraic space and if <MATH_0017> is closed, then\nthe morphism <MATH_0018> should induce an open immersion\n<MATH_0019> which in turn should induce an open immersion\n<MATH_0020>\nAlso, given a formal modification <MATH_0021> of locally Noetherian formal\nalgebraic spaces, the induced morphism <MATH_0022>\nshould be an isomorphism. Given <MATH_0023> and the analytification\nfunctor, we can consider the requirement that\n<MATH_0024>\ncommutes. This makes sense as <MATH_0025>\nis an isomorphism and <MATH_0026>. Finally, under some assumptions\nof faithfulness of the analytification functor, this requirement will\nbe equivalent to the compatibility condition formulated above.\nWe hope this will motivate the reader to think of the compatibility\nof <MATH_0027> and <MATH_0028> as the requirement that some maps be equal,\nrather than asking for the existence of a certain commutative diagram.\n",
      "source_text_hash": "sha256:d048c24b2cff0a1dc8277dc8afefdde91eef89c6ab67e7279635fc4c45b384cc",
